diagnosis_text:
Every diagnostic layer — reachability, L2 identity, ACL, addressing, OSPF control plane, traffic-control, and process inventory — returned clean results with no flags.

**Affected component:** None identified. The user's report describes intermittent link flapping between router_core_1, router_core_2, and router_core_3.

**Mechanism examined:** To confirm or refute the flapping claim, four independent trace sources were checked: (1) carrier_changes counters on every inter-router interface (eth0 and eth1) of all three core routers — each shows exactly 2, which is the startup-only baseline (one DOWN event and one UP event when the container initialised); (2) FRR OSPF daemon logs — zero entries matching neighbour-down, adjacency-change, or dead-interval events; (3) host dmesg — only STP bridge-port initialisation events at a single startup timestamp cluster, nothing post-startup; (4) running process list — no flap script, bounce tool, or ip-link-set-down process present on any device.

**Localization:** All three core routers (router_core_1, router_core_2, router_core_3) are currently fully operational with stable Full OSPF adjacencies and startup-only carrier counters.

**Root cause:** There is no active fault and no historical trace of the reported flapping; the symptom cannot be confirmed from any mechanism or log evidence in the live topology.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required. If the symptom recurs, collect FRR OSPF logs (`show ip ospf neighbor`) and carrier_changes values in real time to capture the transient evidence needed for root-cause isolation.
